22:47:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a great point, and I see exactly where you’re coming from. You’re right—your thermostat isn't lying, and in many houses, switching the furnace fan on doesn’t make a dramatic difference in how 65°F feels. So let me clarify why the car situation is fundamentally different from what you're describing at home, using that exact analogy. ### The key difference: “fan only” vs. “AC on” In your house, when you switch the thermostat to “fan on,” you’re just running the blower. The air isn’t being actively cooled or dehumidified; you’re just recirculating room-temperature air. So, if the house is already 65°F and the humidity is stable, turning on the fan just gently stirs the air. You might feel a slight draft if you’re right under a vent, but it doesn't fundamentally change the *properties* of the air. The temperature and humidity remain the same, so your body loses heat at roughly the same rate. It feels, as you said, still like 65°F. In your car, when you turn the “AC on,” you’re not just turning on a fan. You’re engaging an air conditioning compressor that actively pulls moisture out of the air and cools a coil well below 65°F to wring out that water before the air is blown on you. The air that hits you is drier, and often slightly cooler right at the vent (even if the car’s average temp eventually stabilizes at 65°F). So **car "AC on" at 65°F = dehumidifier + direct cold draft. House "fan on" at 65°F = just gentle recirculation of the same air.** ### But here’s the thing: your house *would* feel different if you changed those hidden factors. Let's stick with your house thermostat. Imagine two 65°F days: - **Scenario A:** It’s a dry winter day, and your furnace fan is just circulating air. Feels normal, maybe a little cool. - **Scenario B:** It’s a muggy summer day, and you have a dehumidifier running in the room *without* changing the thermostat. The room is still 65°F, but the humidity drops from 70% to 40%. You would absolutely feel colder, maybe even chilly, and the dry air would make your skin feel cool. The thermostat would still show 65°F. - **Scenario C:** Same dry air in winter, but you stand directly in front of a powerful fan. You’d feel a cold blast, even though a thermometer on the wall still reads 65°F. Scenario B and C are exactly what your car’s AC is doing simultaneously—dehumidifying and blasting air directly at you—while your house’s “fan on” setting does neither. So it’s not that the thermometer is lying; it’s that the thermometer is blind to humidity and airflow, which are the dominant forces in how your body perceives temperature. So, in a nutshell: you’ve never noticed a difference at home because the "fan" setting doesn’t dehumidify and doesn’t create a directed, high-velocity airstream on your skin. But if you ever run a dehumidifier in a 65°F room, you’ll instantly feel the same "colder" effect as the car AC, thermostat be damned. That’s the hidden half of the equation.
